<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Gaza Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1824 CE – 1895 CE (approx.) · capital Mandlakazi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Nguni conquest state of southern Mozambique founded by Soshangane, the last great African power to resist Portugal in the region.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soshangane">Soshangane</a>, a commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwide">Zwide's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ndwandwe-kingdom">Ndwandwe Kingdom</a>, fled north after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a> defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> and by about 1824 had settled in southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Mozambique</a>. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsonga-people">Tsonga</a> and Chopi communities and defeated Portuguese posts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inhambane">Inhambane</a>, building a kingdom that reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> highlands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> elite ruled through age regiments on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> model, taking tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and young men from conquered peoples, who became known as Shangaan. A long succession war after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soshangane">Soshangane's</a> death in 1858 brought his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mzila">Mzila</a> to power. Later rulers profited from sending men to work on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a> mines.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngungunhane">Ngungunhane</a> moved the capital to Mandlakazi in 1889 and tried to play <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> against Portugal. Portuguese forces defeated his army at Coolela in 1895 and captured him at Chaimite; he died in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a>, and the land became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gaza-empire.jpg" alt="Gaza Empire" width="320" height="489"><figcaption>Image: Ypirétis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_royaume_de_gaza.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1824 CE – 1895 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mandlakazi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Soshangane, Mzila, Ngungunhane</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xitsonga, isiZulu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, <a href="/countries/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gaza Kingdom, Gaza</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1860&amp;focus=gaza-empire" aria-label="Gaza Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5560 2961 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gaza Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5880 3217L5861 3386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5861 3386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5880 3217h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5880 3217h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1860&amp;focus=gaza-empire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
